w. F3 has 3 left, good chance. F4 has 3 left, good. W4 has 4 left, good. W2 has 4 left, good. B2 has 3 left.  \n\nValid actions: Play F3, B2, F4, F2, W4, W2. I should keep tiles that can form melds. I have two F3s, so keeping them gives chance for pung. Two W4s also. B2 is isolated but might form chow with B1/B3? B1: 2 left, B3: 1 left, but B4-B5-B6 already used in meld, so B suit chow possible but not likely. F2 and F4 are singles. Discarding singles might be good. But F2 is wind, and I already have F1 pung, maybe I can aim for Little Three Winds? That requires pungs of two dragons and pair of third dragon? No, Little Three Dragons is for dragons, not winds. Little Four Winds? That requires pungs of three winds and pair of fourth wind. I have F1 pung, two F3s, one F4, one F2. If I get another F3 and F4, I could have F3 pung and F4 pung, and pair F2, that would be Little Four Winds (64 points!) but that's very hard. But possible: I need one more F3 (3 left) and two more F4 (3 left) and one more F2 (1 left) to pair. Unlikely.  \n\nMore realistically, I should aim for a hand with points from Prevalent/Seat Wind pung (4 points) plus maybe Pung of Terminals or Honors from another wind pung (2 points each) or Dragon Pung. I have no dragons. So if I can get another wind pung, that's additional 2 points. E.g., F3 pung gives 2 points for Pung of Terminals or Honors. That would total 6 points, still need 2 more. Could get from Self-Drawn (1) or Closed Wait (1) etc. But not guaranteed.  \n\nMaybe I should try to go for Half Flush (honors + one suit). I have many winds, and my meld is B4-B5-B6, so B suit. If I discard all W tiles, I could keep B and winds. But I have W2 and W4, which are not B. So discard W2 or W4. Also B2 is B suit, keep. So possible to aim for Half Flush: need all tiles to be either honors or B suit. Currently, I have B2, B4-B5-B6 meld, and winds. W2 and W4 are not allowed. So discard W2 or W4 first. Then later discard the other. Half Flush is worth 6 points