 Wind (since both are East Wind) \u2192 that's 2+2 = 4 points.  \n- Need total >= 8 points.\n\nCurrently guaranteed points from exposed melds:  \nF1 pung: Prevalent Wind (2), Seat Wind (2) \u2192 4 points.  \nB4-B5-B6 chow: maybe All Simples (2 points) if rest of hand has no terminals/honors \u2014 but we have F3, F4, T1 terminals/honors, so may not get All Simples. Maybe No Honors? But F1 is honor already exposed, so No Honors impossible.  \n\nFrom hand, if we win with F3 pung: Pung of Terminals or Honors = 2 points.  \nAlso F4 is honor/terminal; if paired, Pung of Terminals or Honors not applicable (only melds), but pair of honors no extra point.  \n\nNeed more: maybe Concealed Hand (2 points) if we win by self-draw and all other melds concealed? But we have two exposed melds already, so not fully concealed.  \nCould aim for All Pungs (6 points): need all 4 melds as pungs/kongs. We have F1 pung, could make F3 pung, need two more pungs from T7 and T1 or T7 and F4? Impossible with current tiles.  \n\nSo likely hand will be low-scoring unless we get high-value patterns.\n\nGiven 3x F3, we can consider discarding F4 or T1 or T7 or T6.  \nT6: only 1 left in wall, harder to complete chow.  \nT7: 4 left, could make T7 pair or T7 pung.  \n\nBut we must avoid discarding tiles that help opponents win.  \nDiscard analysis:  \nF4: only 1 left unseen, safe.  \nT1: terminal, only 1 left unseen, safe.  \nT6: only 1 left unseen.  \nT7: 4 left unseen \u2014 riskier because opponent might need T7 for chow/pung.  \n\nIf I discard T7, I keep F3 pung and have T1 T6 T7 left \u2192 still fragmented.  \nIf I discard T6, keep T7 T7 as possible pair, T1 and F4 as singletons, F3 pung \u2192 then need chow using T1 (requires T2 T3) or F4 pair (draw another F4) \u2192 both unlikely.  \n\nBut goal is to maximize chance of winning while minimizing risk.  \n\n**Evaluation**:  \nBest discard is F4 or T1 because:  \n1. They are less useful for my hand (no immediate meld potential without drawing duplicates, and they are not connecting with other suited tiles).  \n2. Safe from feeding opponents (only 1 copy left each, so low chance opponent is waiting for them).  \n3. Keeping T7 T7 gives me a ready pair if I draw T8 to complete T6 T7 T8 chow, or if I draw another T7 for a pung.  \n\nBetween T1 and F4: F4 is North Wind, not useful for any wind pung unless I draw another, but I already have F3 pung. T1 is terminal bamboo, only chow possibility with T2 T3 (T2 has 4 left, T3 has 4 left), so T1 has slightly more meld potential than F4.  \n\nThus F4 is the least useful.\n\n**Reason**:  \nDiscarding F4 preserves flexibility:  \n- Keep T1 (chance for chow with T2 T3).  \n- Keep T6 T7 T7 (chance for chow with T8, or T7 pair/pung).  \n- Keep F3 pung for meld.  \n- F4 discard is safe (only 1 left unseen).  \nThis maximizes hand improvement chances while minimizing risk.\n\n**Answer**: Play F4."}
